<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.1.xsd">
    <!--
        Three changesets rather than one: MySQL commits every DDL statement on its own, so a changeset that failed half
        way could not simply be run again. Split, each one either ran or did not.
    -->
    <changeSet author="fineract" id="command-00008" context="postgresql,mysql">
        <comment>
            What an idempotency key is unique together with, as the configured CustomIdempotencyDistinctor computes it
            when the key is claimed. The empty string means nothing distinguishes the key, which is what every existing
            row gets and what a command without a key is stored with: not null, so that the composite unique constraint
            below compares two empty values as equal rather than treating two nulls as distinct.
        </comment>
        <addColumn tableName="m_command">
            <column name="custom_idempotency_distinctor" type="varchar(255)" defaultValue="">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>
    <changeSet author="fineract" id="command-00009" context="postgresql,mysql">
        <comment>
            Make the idempotency key unique together with its distinctor. Added before the key-only constraint is
            dropped, so there is no moment at which the key is not unique. Every existing row has the empty distinctor
            and the key-only constraint already holds, so no existing row can violate this one.
        </comment>
        <addUniqueConstraint
                tableName="m_command"
                columnNames="idempotency_key, custom_idempotency_distinctor"
                constraintName="m_command_idempotency_key_distinctor_unique"/>
    </changeSet>
    <changeSet author="fineract" id="command-00010" context="postgresql,mysql">
        <comment>
            Drop the key-only unique constraint, which would otherwise still refuse the same key under a different
            distinctor.
        </comment>
        <dropUniqueConstraint tableName="m_command" constraintName="m_command_idempotency_key_unique"/>
        <rollback>
            <!--
                Fails when a key has since been stored under more than one distinctor: the stricter constraint cannot
                be restored over those rows without deciding which of them to rename, and that is not a rollback's call.
            -->
            <addUniqueConstraint
                    tableName="m_command"
                    columnNames="idempotency_key"
                    constraintName="m_command_idempotency_key_unique"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
